{"causes":["The rise of Romanticism in European literature, which emphasized the importance of emotions and individual experience"],"effects":["The creation of subsequent fairy tales that explore themes of identity and social hierarchy"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Hans Christian Andersen","sourceName":"Hans Christian Andersen","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"The Princess and the Pea","targetName":"The Princess and the Pea","context":"Andersen wrote this fairy tale in 1835"},{"sourceSlug":"European literature","sourceName":"European literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Princess and the Pea","targetName":"The Princess and the Pea","context":"Andersen's work contributed to the rise of Romanticism"},{"sourceSlug":"Romanticism","sourceName":"Romanticism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Fairy tale genre","targetName":"Fairy tale genre","context":"The Princess and the Pea reflects the core principles of Romanticism"},{"sourceSlug":"Journal of Children's Literature","sourceName":"Journal of Children's Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Empathy in children's literature","targetName":"Empathy in children's literature","context":"A study published in the journal found that 75% of children reported feeling empathy for the princess"},{"sourceSlug":"Victorian era","sourceName":"Victorian era","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Princess and the Pea","targetName":"The Princess and the Pea","context":"Andersen wrote The Princess and the Pea during the Victorian era"}],"places":[{"name":"Copenhagen","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Paris","role":"Major publication site"},{"name":"London","role":"Publication venue"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Princess and the Pea","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-princess-and-the-pea"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11861","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Princess_and_the_Pea"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Princess and the Pea contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Princess and the Pea (Danish: Prinsessen på ærten) is a fairy tale by Hans Christian Andersen (1805–1875), first published on 8 May 1835 in the first instalment of Eventyr fortalte for Børn ('Fairy Tales Told for Children') — one of Andersen's first published fairy tales, alongside The Tinderbox, Little Claus and Big Claus, and Thumbelina. The tale narrates the story of a prince who wants to marry a real princess but cannot find one; when a bedraggled young woman arrives at the palace claiming to be a princess, the queen tests her authenticity by placing a single pea under twenty mattresses and twenty feather beds; the woman is unable to sleep for the discomfort of the pea, and her extreme sensitivity is taken as proof of her royal authenticity.

The Princess and the Pea is one of Andersen's shortest tales — a single page in its original form — and is unusual in his canon for being entirely ironic: the test for royal authenticity is the ability to feel a pea through twenty mattresses and twenty feather beds, which is extreme sensitivity rather than any moral, intellectual, or practical quality. The tale's satirical implication — that aristocratic refinement is a kind of hypersensitivity, an absurd over-cultivation of delicacy — has been widely noted, though Andersen may have intended the tale as a straightforward fairy tale rather than social satire.

The Princess and the Pea has entered global culture as an idiom for extreme sensitivity — describing someone who is 'a princess and the pea' suggests hypersensitivity, excessive delicacy, or complaint about trivial discomforts. Andersen based the tale on a motif from oral folk tradition (a sensitivity test for true royalty exists in several European folk traditions), but his version is the most widely known. The tale has been adapted in musical theatre (Once Upon a Mattress, 1959, which ran on Broadway for two years), opera, and film, and its central image of the pea under the mattresses is one of the most immediately recognisable in the Andersen canon.","subjects":["Danish Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Hans Christian Andersen","Children's Literature","Satire","Folk Literature","Musical Theatre"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Princess and the Pea has had a lasting impact on readers of all ages, exploring themes of identity and social hierarchy through its unique narrative style and use of symbolism.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
